00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:25.060
scenario. On March 15th, you sold 200 shares of a Canadian mining name inside your TFSA after it

00:06:25.060 --> 00:06:33.620
dropped 12%. The order filled at $18.40, booking what looked like a clean $480 capital loss you plan

00:06:33.620 --> 00:06:39.660
to use to offset gains in your non-registered account. 18 days later, you repurchased the identical shares,

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:46.640
but this time inside your RRSP. The CRA applied the superficial loss rule and denied the entire tax

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:52.520
benefit. The rule is brutally simple. If you or an affiliated person acquire an identical property

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:59.700
30 days before or after the sale, the capital loss is disallowed. That creates a 61-day window that is

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:05.260
completely unforgiving, buying in a different account through a spouse or even inside a corporation

00:07:05.260 --> 00:07:12.660
you control can trigger it. In the current 2025 to 2026 tax environment, with the inclusion rate

00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:23.900
at 66.67% above $250, a denied $10, capital loss cost you roughly $2, 222 in immediate tax deferral,

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:30.380
and that's before compounding is considered. The fix is elegant, but requires discipline. Professionals

00:07:30.380 --> 00:07:37.140
maintain a substitute basket of highly correlated but non-identical securities. Same sector, similar

00:07:37.140 --> 00:07:43.440
beta, different ticker. You stay invested while the 61-day clock runs, then rotate back if you wish.

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:49.180
Before every sale, check the adjusted cost-based tracker in your brokerage portal. Run a 61-day

00:07:49.180 --> 00:07:54.320
forward calendar check and document your substitute names in advance. The single biggest mistake is

00:07:54.320 --> 00:07:59.920
assuming that account silos protect you. They don't. This one mechanic, proper loss harvesting with

00:07:59.920 --> 00:08:04.920
substitute securities, prevents more surprise tax bills than any other retail error I've seen.

